<p>If you are waiting on an insurance decision after a storm, you are not alone. Understanding <strong>why property insurance claims are delayed in Texas</strong> starts with knowing what the law expects of the insurer, and what the insurer can fairly expect of you. Most delays fall into a few predictable categories. Many can be shortened with the right paperwork and a clear written record.</p>

<p>This article walks through the Texas prompt-payment deadlines, the separate TWIA claim timeline that applies to many coastal properties in Corpus Christi and the Coastal Bend, the usual reasons claims stall, and what you can do about it. It is general information, not legal advice. For questions about lawsuits, delay damages, or deadlines to sue, consult a Texas attorney.</p>

<h2>The Texas prompt-payment deadlines (Chapter 542)</h2>

<p>The Prompt Payment of Claims Act, in Subchapter B of Texas Insurance Code Chapter 542, applies to most first-party property claims with private insurers. It sets a sequence of deadlines. Each step starts a new clock, which is why it helps to note the date of every letter and email.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Deadline</th><th>Statute</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Acknowledge the claim, begin investigating, request needed items</td><td>15 days after notice of claim (30 business days for eligible surplus lines insurers)</td><td>§542.055</td></tr>
<tr><td>Accept or reject the claim</td><td>15 business days after receiving all requested items (30 days if arson is suspected)</td><td>§542.056</td></tr>
<tr><td>If more time is needed</td><td>Insurer must explain why within that period, then decide within 45 days of that notice</td><td>§542.056</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pay the claim</td><td>5 business days after notice of acceptance (20 business days for surplus lines)</td><td>§542.057</td></tr>
<tr><td>Delay damages may apply</td><td>When payment is delayed beyond the statutory period, or more than 60 days if no other statute sets a period</td><td>§542.058</td></tr>
<tr><td>Weather catastrophe extension</td><td>Deadlines extended 15 days after a catastrophe or major natural disaster as defined by the Commissioner</td><td>§542.059(b)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Two details in that table explain a lot of frustration. First, the 15-business-day decision period does not start until the insurer receives everything it asked for. Second, the insurer can extend that period by giving you written notice of the reason, then has 45 days from that notice to decide. A claim can therefore be "on time" under the statute even when it feels slow to you.</p>

<p>Use our <a href="/claim-deadline-calculator/">claim deadline calculator</a> to map these dates against your own claim. Enter the date you reported the loss and the dates of key letters, and you will see which deadlines may already have passed.</p>

<h2>TWIA claims follow a different timeline</h2>

<p>Nueces, San Patricio, Aransas, and Kleberg counties are all inside TWIA's designated catastrophe area, and TWIA covers wind and hail only. If your wind or hail claim is with TWIA, the deadlines come from Chapter 2210, Subchapter L-1, which controls over other laws that conflict with it.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>TWIA step</th><th>Deadline</th><th>Statute</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>File the claim</td><td>No later than one year after the date the damage occurs; the Commissioner may extend by up to 180 days for good cause</td><td>§2210.573(a), §2210.205</td></tr>
<tr><td>TWIA requests missing information</td><td>Within 30 days after the claim is filed</td><td>§2210.573(b)</td></tr>
<tr><td>TWIA accepts in full, in part, or denies</td><td>Within 60 days of receiving the claim or the requested information, whichever is later</td><td>§2210.573(d)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Extensions</td><td>The Commissioner may extend; extensions of TWIA-only deadlines for claims from one event are capped at 120 days total</td><td>§2210.581</td></tr>
<tr><td>Payment of accepted amounts</td><td>Within 10 days after the notice (or after a required act by the claimant)</td><td>§2210.5731</td></tr>
<tr><td>Demand appraisal over the amount</td><td>Within 60 days after receiving the accept or partial-accept notice</td><td>§2210.574(b)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>TWIA's dispute process also has its own steps for denied coverage, including a notice of intent to sue and a two-year statute of repose that runs from the denial notice. Those are legal deadlines, and missing them can waive rights, so talk with a Texas attorney if you are considering a coverage dispute. Our guide to the <a href="/twia-claim-process-corpus-christi/">TWIA claim process in Corpus Christi</a> covers the full sequence, and our <a href="/services/twia-windstorm-claims/">TWIA claim help</a> page explains where a public adjuster fits.</p>

<h2>Common reasons Texas property claims get delayed</h2>

<p>Delays rarely come from a single cause. In practice, we see the same handful of issues over and over on Coastal Bend claims.</p>

<h3>1. Missing or incomplete documents</h3>
<p>Because the decision clock often waits on "all items requested," a single missing invoice, proof-of-loss form, or set of photos can hold up the whole claim. Sometimes the request was sent to an old email address or buried in a portal message. Ask the adjuster for a written list of everything still outstanding.</p>

<h3>2. Catastrophe volume</h3>
<p>After a hurricane, a tropical storm, or a widespread event like the May 8, 2025 windstorm that NWS surveys estimated at 105 to 115 mph gusts around Petronila, Driscoll, and Agua Dulce, insurers may receive thousands of claims in a short time. Field adjusters are stretched thin, reinspections stack up, and the 15-day catastrophe extension under §542.059 may apply.</p>

<h3>3. Engineer and specialist reports</h3>
<p>Insurers sometimes hire engineers to evaluate roof, structural, or foundation damage. Scheduling, inspecting, and writing those reports takes time, and the insurer may not decide until the report is in. Ask for a copy of any report the decision relies on.</p>

<h3>4. Wind vs. water questions</h3>
<p>Many coastal owners carry a homeowners policy that excludes wind and hail, a TWIA policy, and a flood policy. When a hurricane brings both wind and surge, each insurer may wait to see what the other covers. Our article on <a href="/wind-vs-storm-surge-damage-claims-texas-coast/">wind vs. storm surge damage claims</a> explains why these disputes are common and how to document them.</p>

<h3>5. Disagreement over scope or price</h3>
<p>When your contractor's estimate and the insurer's estimate are far apart, the claim can sit in back-and-forth for months. A clear, line-item estimate in the same format as the carrier's helps both sides see exactly where they differ.</p>

<h3>6. Adjuster changes and reassignment</h3>
<p>Claims are sometimes handed from a field adjuster to a desk adjuster, or reassigned during busy periods. Each handoff can mean a new person learning the file. A written summary of the claim, with dates and attachments, shortens that learning curve.</p>

<h3>7. Payment logistics after the decision</h3>
<p>Even after a claim is accepted, money can be slow to reach the repair. If a mortgage company is named on the check, it may hold the funds and release them in stages through its loss draft department, often after its own inspection. Checks may also list more than one payee. Ask your lender early what it needs, and confirm that the insurer has the correct mailing address and payee names. These delays are not covered by the same deadlines as the claim decision itself, so plan for them.</p>

<h3>8. Delays that start on the policyholder side</h3>
<p>Some delays are unintentional and self-inflicted. A late report of the loss, a missed reinspection appointment, or an unreturned proof-of-loss form can all push the timeline back. So can repairs made before the insurer had a chance to see the damage, without photos to show what was there. None of this means the claim is lost, but it gives the insurer a fair reason to wait. Responding promptly and keeping copies of everything you send is the simplest fix.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>What you can do to keep a delayed claim moving</h2>

<p>You cannot control the insurer's workload, but you can remove the reasons a claim might legitimately wait. These steps are practical and low cost.</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>Get the outstanding-items list in writing.</strong> Ask the adjuster by email: "Please list every item, statement, or form you still need to make a decision on this claim." That answer can define when the decision clock starts.</li>
<li><strong>Keep a contact log.</strong> Record every call, email, inspection, and letter with the date, the person, and what was said. Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> includes a simple format.</li>
<li><strong>Send documents in one organized package.</strong> Label photos by area, include dates, and attach invoices and estimates in a single submission so nothing gets lost.</li>
<li><strong>Follow up in writing on a schedule.</strong> A short, polite status request every week or two keeps the claim in front of the adjuster and creates a record.</li>
<li><strong>Use official channels.</strong> If a claim remains stalled, you can file a complaint with the Texas Department of Insurance. TDI also runs an ombudsman program to help TWIA policyholders with the claims process.</li>
<li><strong>Protect the damaged property.</strong> Reasonable temporary repairs, such as a roof tarp or water extraction, help prevent further damage while you wait. Keep receipts and photos.</li>
</ol>

<h3>When a delay becomes a legal question</h3>

<p>If a claim has been unreasonably delayed, Texas law may allow delay damages under §542.058, and Chapter 542A generally requires written notice at least 61 days before filing suit against a private insurer. Chapter 542A does not apply to suits against TWIA, which has its own process. These are legal matters. A public adjuster is not an attorney and cannot give legal advice, so talk with a Texas attorney if you are thinking about suit or are unsure about a deadline.</p>

<h2>How a public adjuster helps with delayed claims</h2>

<p>Corpus Christi Public Adjuster works for the policyholder. On a delayed claim, that usually means reviewing the claim file and correspondence, identifying what the insurer is still waiting on, preparing a complete estimate and documentation package, and handling the follow-up in writing. When the delay is really a disagreement over the amount of loss, we can help evaluate whether appraisal is worth considering.</p>

<p>We cannot promise a faster decision or a particular result. What we can do is take the avoidable delays off the table. Serving Corpus Christi, Nueces County, and surrounding Coastal Bend communities, Corpus Christi Public Adjuster handles both private-insurer and TWIA claims, so we track the right set of deadlines for each policy. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

